{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And he said, etc. — 'Now let me die,' etc.</b> The reason he said <b>הַפָּעַם</b> ('this time') is: because he had said (Genesis 37:35) ״כִּי אֵרֵד אֶל בְּנִי אָבֵל שְׁאוֹלָה״, now he said he would not see the grave." "Further, it appears to learn from the precision of his saying <b>כִּי עוֹדְךָ חָי</b> ('that you are still alive') — and he did not suffice to say ״אַחֲרֵי רְאוֹתִי פָּנֶיךָ״, and the matter is self-evident, for he is alive. Also, one needs to know why Jacob did not say this when the brothers informed him that Joseph was alive — and we find that he believed when he saw the wagons (Genesis 45:26) ״וַתְּחִי רוּחַ״, etc. — so what new thing did he see?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of Jacob's words is that although he was informed that he was still alive, this sufficed only to know that he exists in reality; but his heart was still faint over him, on account of his being among the nations — a lowly slave ruled by others — [wondering] whether he was still in his righteousness and his eye had not dimmed and his spirit had not fled; or on account of his being separated from him and given over within the klipot, and especially the impurity of Egypt; and all the more so on account of what had departed from the holy spirit, as he had said (Genesis 37) ״טָרֹף טֹרַף יוֹסֵף חַיָּה רָעָה אֲכָלָתְהוּ״ (Genesis Rabbah, ch. 84). And it is a well-known thing that the righteous far more desire [to know] the state of the son when he is a son [of theirs], and all the more so Jacob the righteous. Therefore his joy was not complete because of this doubt, until he saw him and recognized in his face — for recognition of the face testifies about the man. And so we find regarding complete righteous ones and also many others who recognized through the mark of the face the quality of a person, and all the more so Jacob our father, who would recognize. Therefore when he recognized him, he said to him ״אָמוּתָה הַפָּעַם״ — meaning, this time, in seeing you, and not previously when I was informed. And the reason <b>אַחֲרֵי רְאוֹתִי פָּנֶיךָ</b> — and I recognized in them that <b>you are still alive</b> — meaning, righteous as before, and the form of your face has not changed from what it was, but you are still alive; for the righteous are called 'alive' (Berakhot 18a). And this is correct."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {2 "In the phrase בִּהְיוֹתוֹ בֵּן מֵבִישׁ, the text is slightly difficult; translated hyper-literally as 'when he is a son [of theirs],' reading בֵּן as 'son' with the following clause understood as the father's concern for his own child. A variant reading may be intended."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:19.328141796Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}